12:["$","article",null,{"children":[["$","$L1c",null,{"topic":{"id":"grief-and-loss","title":"Grief & Loss: Carrying What Cannot Be Fixed","subtitle":"Grief is not a staircase you climb. It is weather you learn to live inside.","category":"Grief & Loss","tone":"blush","icon":"heartCrack","keywords":["grief","bereavement","loss","mourning","anniversary","death"],"readingTime":7,"featuredImage":"/89ea5304-a8bb-41e5-9dea-1e2b10aaa20c.jpg","excerpt":"The five stages were never meant as a sequence. Modern grief care focuses on oscillation — moving between loss and life — rather than on reaching acceptance.","reviewer":"Reviewed by Renée Alvarez, LCSW","updated":"Updated August 2026","intro":["Grief is not only about death. It follows divorce, diagnosis, estrangement, miscarriage, and the loss of a future you had assumed. Any of those can produce the full physical weight of mourning.","The five-stage model was originally observed in dying patients, not the bereaved, and was never intended as a checklist. Grief does not progress tidily, and revisiting an earlier feeling is not regression."],"mechanics":{"heading":"Oscillation, not stages","body":["Current models describe grief as moving back and forth between confronting the loss and attending to ongoing life. Both are necessary. A morning of laughter does not undo mourning, and a wave of sorrow two years on is not a relapse.","Grief also lives in the body: appetite changes, disrupted sleep, chest heaviness, and a real hit to concentration and immune function. This is why practical care — food, water, sleep — is legitimate grief work."]},"symptoms":[{"label":"Waves rather than a slope","description":"Intensity arrives suddenly, often triggered by something small and ordinary.","kind":"Mind"},{"label":"Physical heaviness","description":"Chest pressure, aching limbs, and exhaustion that sleep does not touch.","kind":"Body"},{"label":"Searching behaviour","description":"Scanning crowds, rereading messages, keeping a phone number active.","kind":"Behavior"},{"label":"Guilt and rehearsal","description":"Replaying what might have been said or done differently.","kind":"Mind"}],"myths":[{"myth":"Grief lasts about a year.","fact":"There is no schedule. Acute grief often softens over months, but adaptation continues for years and anniversaries stay tender."},{"myth":"Staying busy is the healthiest approach.","fact":"Distraction has a place, but grief that is never approached tends to surface as physical symptoms or sudden collapse."},{"myth":"Moving forward means letting go.","fact":"Most people keep a continuing bond with the person they lost. Integration, not erasure, is the goal."}],"strategies":[{"name":"Continuing-bond letters","summary":"Keeps the relationship present in a form you control.","steps":["Write to them, not about them — present tense.","Include ordinary news, not only sorrow.","Stop when it becomes heavy; there is no requirement to finish."]},{"name":"Anniversary planning","summary":"Removes the dread of being ambushed by a date.","steps":["Mark the date and decide in advance who you want nearby.","Choose one small ritual — a walk, a meal they loved, a candle.","Give yourself explicit permission to cancel everything else."]},{"name":"Baseline care card","summary":"Reduces daily decisions to a short, achievable list.","steps":["Write four items: water, one meal, outside air, one message answered.","Keep it visible in the kitchen.","Treat a partly ticked card as a completed day."]}],"treatments":[{"name":"Grief-focused counselling","abbreviation":"GC","description":"Individual or group support that normalises the process and helps rebuild routine and identity after a loss."},{"name":"Complicated Grief Treatment","abbreviation":"CGT","description":"A structured therapy for prolonged grief disorder, where intense yearning persists past twelve months and blocks daily function."},{"name":"Medical review","abbreviation":"MD","description":"Useful when sleep, appetite, or chest symptoms persist, both to treat them and to rule out physical causes."}],"support":{"guidance":"Say the person’s name. Grieving people are usually more afraid of the loss being forgotten than of being reminded of it. Show up on the ordinary Tuesday, not only at the funeral.","say":["“I remember when she used to do this — I still think about it.”","“I have no words that help, but I am not going anywhere.”","“I am coming over Saturday to do the laundry, not to talk.”"],"avoid":["“They are in a better place now.”","“Everything happens for a reason.”","“At least you had many years together.”"]},"faqs":[{"question":"When is grief a clinical concern?","answer":"If after twelve months there is persistent, intense yearning with an inability to function, prolonged grief disorder may apply and specific treatment helps."},{"question":"Should children attend a funeral?","answer":"Usually yes, if they want to and are prepared for what they will see. Exclusion tends to increase confusion rather than reduce distress."},{"question":"Why do I feel relief alongside sorrow?","answer":"Relief is extremely common, especially after long illness or a difficult relationship. It coexists with love rather than contradicting it."}],"pacer":[{"label":"Even 5-0-5","inhale":5,"hold":0,"exhale":5,"description":"No hold — useful when crying makes breath-holding uncomfortable."},{"label":"Gentle 4-4-6","inhale":4,"hold":4,"exhale":6,"description":"For the tight-chested moments that arrive without warning."}]}}],"$L1d","$L1e"]}]
